Incremental Syntax Architecture
===============================================================================

Status: Phase 1 experimental architecture.

Integration branch: `feature/syntax`.

The branch combines PR 650 at
`45a8acf1d1ee457a40340f17b9dc009ae781dfce` with the completed Phase 0
canonical-grammar merge at
`9511d4d0c9af7893d7230aa98c792f705d504565`.

The authoritative canonical inventory is
`docs/design/grammar-audit/productions.tsv`; it contains exactly 539 canonical
rules. A deterministic developer script generates the checked-in
`src/syntax/src/document/parser/canonical_rules.rs` registry. Production
package builds read only that package-local registry; a repository test
recomputes and verifies every ordered `(name, RuleId)` entry against the
inventory.

Current canonical parity progress is 328 / 539 productions. The closed
Phase 2H structure-shell layer contributes 10 of those productions without
adding a complete matrix, table, map, set, structure, expression, or document
parser root.

The exact next executable implementation boundary is generated from the
normative `ebnf:canonical` grammar graph in `docs/design/specification.mec` and
recorded in `docs/design/grammar-audit/phase-2i-recursive-core.tsv`.
`canonical-dependencies.tsv` records the generated canonical edges,
`productions.tsv` retains inventory and implementation-derived legacy call
provenance, and `ports.tsv` defines migration status. The legacy call columns do
not define the canonical grammar closure.

Phase 2I-A adds only the generated dependency contract and ports no production.
The recursive parser itself remains absent.

The legacy parser, public `parse` API, existing `Program` AST, formatter,
interpreter, runtime, and accepted grammar remain unchanged while this
architecture is introduced.

1. Goals
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The canonical parsed representation of one source revision is a lossless
`SyntaxSnapshot`. It is designed for whole `.mec` documents, live incomplete
input, multiple independent errors, streamed appends, incremental edits, exact
Unicode preservation, structured diagnostics, and later partial semantic
analysis.

The persisted canonical artifact remains UTF-8 `.mec` text. The parsed form is
revisioned derived state.

This phase deliberately implements a narrow prototype grammar slice. Partial
productions use `prototype-*` `ParserContextId` values rather than claiming
canonical `RuleId` identities. It does not port or change the complete
canonical grammar.

2. Documents, not a host/guest format
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Document mode always parses sibling Mech and Mechdown constructs. Mechdown is
not an optional wrapper, and parsing does not try a complete Mech program before
falling back to prose.

The document root contains sections and document items. Items may be paragraphs,
Mech items, canonical `subtitle` and `ul-subtitle` headings, lists, tables,
fences, equations, figures, Mika, and other Mechdown forms as their grammar
families are ported.

Tools may use fragment entry points for isolated productions, but `.mec` always
selects document mode.

3. Total parsing
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

```rust
pub fn parse_document(
  source: TextSnapshot,
  config: ParseConfig,
) -> SyntaxSnapshot;
```

Every valid UTF-8 input produces a snapshot. User syntax errors do not produce a
top-level `Err`. On resource exhaustion, the parser retains every token and
completed node emitted through `covered_end`, appends one `ERROR` node for only
`covered_end .. parse_range.end`, and closes open nodes with dynamically
reserved emergency events. Scanner cursor progress is deliberately not
coverage: bytes from an unfinished token scanner remain in the error remainder
until a real source token event has been accepted.

Preserving a document prefix requires at least seven events: the two enclosing
document starts, a three-event error envelope, and the corresponding enclosing
finishes. `max_events` below this documented minimum permits an explicit,
lossless whole-range fallback. At and above the minimum, whole-range fallback
is not used merely because the parser exhausts its event budget.

Strict validation and lowering are later consumers:

```rust
parse_document(source, config)
validate_strict(&snapshot)
lower_live(&snapshot)
```

The parser kernel is an event-based ordinary-function parser. It uses explicit
checkpoints, commitment, recovery, rule context, and fuel. It does not use Nom,
boxed parser closures, a parser generator, or an external tree framework.

4. Revisioned source
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

UTF-8 byte offsets are the primary coordinate system. Rows, byte columns,
UTF-16 positions, grapheme positions, and display widths are derived views.
Parser cursors store byte offsets only.

A parser cursor has separate consumption and context boundaries. Document
cursors set both boundaries to the physical source end. Fragment cursors start
at the requested global range, set `consume_end` to that range's end, and set
`context_end` to the physical source end. All ordinary peek and bump operations
remain bounded by `consume_end`. Explicit immutable context-lookahead operations
may inspect through `context_end` for diagnostic `FoundSyntax` and strong
heading or fence boundary recognition, but expose no operation that can advance
the parser or emit a token outside the fragment range.

A `TextSnapshot` identifies a `DocumentId` and `Revision`. Its immutable piece
list references `Arc<str>` chunks and byte ranges within those chunks. Edits
reuse unchanged chunks. Append is insertion at EOF. Multiple edits in one
revision are sorted, non-overlapping, and expressed in the old revision.

Edits are rejected when a range is reversed, outside the snapshot, or not on a
UTF-8 boundary. Input is never normalized. LF, CRLF, and CR remain byte-exact
and are all recognized by the derived line index.

The current list representation intentionally leaves cursor and tree interfaces
independent of storage shape so a balanced representation can replace it later.

5. Lossless green and red trees
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Green nodes and tokens are immutable. They store relative lengths, stable
session-minted identities, flags, structural hashes, and child arrays. They do
not store absolute offsets or redundant token strings. A red view combines a
green element with a source revision and derives its range from ancestor and
sibling lengths.

The lossless invariants are:

- Every source byte belongs to exactly one non-synthetic token.
- Concatenating non-synthetic token text reproduces the source byte-for-byte.
- Whitespace, comments, and delimiters are tokens.
- Missing tokens have zero width and are synthetic.
- Skipped malformed tokens remain below an `ERROR` node.
- The tree does not globally intern identical subtrees.

`NodeId` and `TokenId` are minted by a document session. A reused green subtree
keeps its identities. A reparsed subtree receives new identities. Hashes support
comparison and caching but never define editor identity.

The `NodeIndex` resolves each identity to its current kind, range, parent, and
flags. This supports red navigation and diagnostic anchoring without putting
absolute offsets in green storage.

Typed AST views are zero-copy wrappers over red nodes. Accessors return `Option`
because live syntax may omit required children.

6. Structural errors and diagnostics
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Absent required syntax is represented by zero-width `MISSING` nodes or tokens.
Unexpected source is retained as ordinary tokens under an `ERROR` node.

Diagnostic messages are not stored in green nodes. A revisioned
`DiagnosticStore` contains stable namespaced codes, phases, severity, canonical
rule IDs, internal parser-context IDs, structural or absolute anchors, labels,
expected and found syntax, fixes, related diagnostics, recovery actions, tags,
and display messages. Canonical rule IDs are generated only from the Phase 0
inventory; recovery machinery and partial prototype productions cannot mint
canonical-looking IDs.

Structural anchors use an element identity plus a relative range. When an
unchanged node shifts after an earlier edit, its diagnostics shift with it.
Absolute anchors are revision-specific and are used when no suitable syntax
identity exists.

The stable fields, rather than English wording, are the test oracle. Optional
Serde support supplies JSON for tools. A small plain-text renderer supplies
human-readable output without coupling the schema to a terminal library.

7. Recovery
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Productions distinguish `NoMatch`, `Match`, and `CommittedFailure`. Once a
distinctive prefix such as a define operator is recognized, the construct may
recover but cannot fall back to unrelated prose.

Recovery operations are:

- Insert a missing element at a legitimate boundary.
- Skip unexpected source into an `ERROR` node.
- Abandon an incomplete production to an ancestor restart root.

Every parser and recovery loop consumes a source byte, inserts once and changes
grammar state, returns, or terminates. Fuel, nesting, event, diagnostic, and
recovery-byte limits enforce this invariant.

Recovery uses contextual restart entries. Document, paragraph, Mech, and fence
modes record delimiter depth, line-start state, and indentation. Canonical
headings, matching fence boundaries, and delimiter-aware Mech terminals form
strong restart points. Recovery never treats a Markdown `#` convention as a
Mechdown heading.

`ParseContext.delimiter_depth` is the number of delimiter-owning ancestors
already open before a fragment root begins. A top-level parenthetical fragment
therefore starts at depth zero; the inner parenthetical in `((1))` starts at
depth one, and the next nested parenthetical starts at depth two. Restart-index
construction counts actual delimiter-owning ancestors rather than assigning a
constant depth. The fragment parser initializes its nesting counter from this
context before parsing the fragment's own opening delimiter.

8. Incremental reparsing
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Edits create a `ChangeMap`, locate the smallest intersecting restart root, and
expand when boundaries, newlines, composite sigils, delimiters, fence prefixes,
headings, or relevant indentation change.

A fragment parser operates on a bounded global range of the original
`TextSnapshot`. It never copies source into a second snapshot with the same
document identity. `FragmentKind` selects the exact production entry point, and
`ParseContext` carries mode, delimiter depth, line-start state, indentation, and
an enclosing fence delimiter. Fragment tokens and diagnostics retain global
source ranges. Non-consuming right-context lookahead lets a fragment ending
immediately before a heading or fence report the same found syntax and recovery
data as a containing full-document parse without relaxing its consumption
boundary.

A fragment splice is accepted only when that entry point matches, consumes the
complete mapped range, preserves the ending boundary, and retains parser mode.
For a non-section splice, the implementation conservatively reparses the
containing section after constructing the candidate snapshot and merging
retained and fragment diagnostics. Acceptance requires both the section tree
and flags and the complete normalized diagnostics whose primary ranges belong
to that section to match. Normalization includes code, phase, severity,
canonical rule, parser context, primary and label ranges, expected and found
syntax, fixes and edits, related diagnostics, recovery, and tags; related
indices are rebuilt within the filtered section result.

Otherwise the reparser ascends. Document-root reparsing is the final fallback.
Snapshots whose diagnostic budget was exhausted also use the document fallback,
because a local fragment cannot reconstruct diagnostics suppressed outside its
range.

Unaffected green subtrees are reused with their IDs. Full and incremental parses
are compared after normalization that ignores minted IDs.

Incremental statistics account for every parser invocation performed by an
operation. They report selected fragment work, containing-section validation
work, rejected fragment or parent-root attempts, and document fallback work
separately. `total_parser_steps` and `total_events_emitted` are their sums;
the legacy `parser_steps` and `events_emitted` fields are aliases for those
totals. Because section-envelope validation is intentionally conservative, a
one-section local edit may perform as much or more parser work than a full
parse. Multi-section edits should still demonstrate less total work when
unchanged sections are reused.

9. Annotation substrate and future analysis
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Syntax annotations use typed `NodeMap<T>` values at one revision. Reusing a
syntax node permits its annotation to be retained; replacing or deleting the
node invalidates the entry. This is a generic typed map, not a dynamic property
bag.

Most future semantic facts attach to HIR because one syntax node may lower to
multiple semantic entities. Origin mappings project them back to syntax:

```rust
pub struct AnalysisSnapshot {
  pub document: DocumentId,
  pub revision: Revision,

  pub origins: HirMap<SyntaxPtr>,
  pub kinds: HirMap<FactState<KindInfo>>,
  pub dimensions: HirMap<FactState<DimensionInfo>>,
  pub effects: HirMap<FactState<EffectInfo>>,
  pub coeffects: HirMap<FactState<CoeffectInfo>>,
  pub refinements: HirMap<FactState<RefinementInfo>>,
  pub liveness: HirMap<FactState<LivenessInfo>>,
}
```

`mech-syntax` supplies reusable identities, `SyntaxPtr`, `NodeMap<T>`, and
`FactState<T>`. It does not define placeholder semantic fact types.

10. Portability
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Production document modules use `core` and `alloc`, forbid unsafe code, and do
not access filesystems, threads, clocks, terminal coloring, or platform APIs.
Observable maps are deterministic. Source and diagnostic serialization is
behind the existing `serde` feature.

The design remains implementable in Mech: piece arrays, immutable nodes, event
arrays, typed maps, checkpoints, and explicit recovery are concrete data
structures rather than a Rust-specific framework.

11. Structural presentation retention
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Concrete syntax retains presentation exactly: delimiters, border spellings,
horizontal trivia, and physical newlines remain ordinary syntax tokens. Semantic
structures intentionally discard that presentation when compatibility lowering
constructs values such as matrices, maps, sets, and table rows.

Format preservation is therefore syntax-driven. A formatter may preserve the
retained structure or apply an explicitly selected normalization policy, but it
must not infer an original presentation from semantic dimensions, row counts,
estimated width, or similar heuristics. Detached presentation metadata is not
used because it could become stale after an incremental edit.
